#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720387 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the section of $y=\\ln{\\left(x^{2} + 2 \\right)}$ with $x>0$ between its intersections with the horizontal lines $y=\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}$ and $y=\\ln{\\left(18 \\right)}$.\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(2 x e^{y} + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nusing $x=t$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "First solve the intersection equations on the specified branch:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln(x^{2} + 2)=\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}&\\implies x^{2} + 2=3\\implies x=1\\\\\n\\ln(x^{2} + 2)=\\ln{\\left(18 \\right)}&\\implies x^{2} + 2=18\\implies x=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet $x=t$. Parametrize the curve by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad 1\\le t\\le 4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates and take the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle 1,\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\right)^2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=2 t e^{\\left(\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)} + 2 \\left(\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=2 t^{3} + 4 t + 2 \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $e^{\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}}=t^{2} + 2$ because the logarithm argument is positive on the interval.\n\nMultiply the scalar by the speed and use the parameter bounds:\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{1}^{4} f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThus the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{4}\\left(2 t^{3} + 4 t + 2 \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720394 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the section of $y=\\ln{\\left(x^{2} + 2 \\right)}$ with $x>0$ between its intersections with the horizontal lines $y=\\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}$ and $y=\\ln{\\left(18 \\right)}$.\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(- x e^{y} + y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nusing $x=t$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "First solve the intersection equations on the specified branch:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln(x^{2} + 2)=\\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}&\\implies x^{2} + 2=6\\implies x=2\\\\\n\\ln(x^{2} + 2)=\\ln{\\left(18 \\right)}&\\implies x^{2} + 2=18\\implies x=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet $x=t$. Parametrize the curve by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad 2\\le t\\le 4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates and take the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle 1,\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\right)^2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=- t e^{\\left(\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)} + \\left(\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=- t^{3} - 2 t + \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $e^{\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}}=t^{2} + 2$ because the logarithm argument is positive on the interval.\n\nMultiply the scalar by the speed and use the parameter bounds:\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{2}^{4} f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThus the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\int_{2}^{4}\\left(- t^{3} - 2 t + \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720402 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the section of $y=2 x + 2$ between its intersections with the horizontal lines $y=6$ and $y=8$.\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(y e^{x}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nusing $x=t$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "First solve the intersection equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 x + 2=6&\\implies x=2\\\\\n2 x + 2=8&\\implies x=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet $x=t$. Parametrize the curve by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,2 t + 2\\rangle,\\qquad 2\\le t\\le 3.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates and take the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle 1,2\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{1+\\left(2\\right)^2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=\\left(2 t + 2\\right) e^{t}\\\\\n&=2 \\left(t + 1\\right) e^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the scalar by the speed and use the parameter bounds:\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{2}^{3} f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThus the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\int_{2}^{3}\\left(2 \\left(t + 1\\right) e^{t}\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(2\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720403 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the section of $y=2 e^{x}$ between its intersections with the horizontal lines $y=2$ and $y=2 e$.\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nusing $x=t$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "First solve the intersection equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 e^{x}=2&\\implies x=0\\\\\n2 e^{x}=2 e&\\implies x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet $x=t$. Parametrize the curve by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,2 e^{t}\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates and take the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle 1,2 e^{t}\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{1+\\left(2 e^{t}\\right)^2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=t \\left(2 e^{t}\\right)\\\\\n&=2 t e^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the scalar by the speed and use the parameter bounds:\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{0}^{1} f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThus the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(2 t e^{t}\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(2 e^{t}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720404 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the section of $y=x^{2}$ with $x<0$ between its intersections with the horizontal lines $y=16$ and $y=1$.\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(- 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nusing $x=t$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "First solve the intersection equations on the specified branch:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^{2}=16&\\implies x=-4\\\\\nx^{2}=1&\\implies x=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet $x=t$. Parametrize the curve by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,t^{2}\\rangle,\\qquad -4\\le t\\le -1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates and take the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle 1,2 t\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{1+\\left(2 t\\right)^2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=- 2 \\left(t^{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=- 2 t^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the scalar by the speed and use the parameter bounds:\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{-4}^{-1} f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThus the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\int_{-4}^{-1}\\left(- 2 t^{2}\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(2 t\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720409 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the section of $y=3 x + 1$ between its intersections with the horizontal lines $y=4$ and $y=7$.\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(y e^{x}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nusing $x=t$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "First solve the intersection equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 x + 1=4&\\implies x=1\\\\\n3 x + 1=7&\\implies x=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet $x=t$. Parametrize the curve by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,3 t + 1\\rangle,\\qquad 1\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates and take the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle 1,3\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{1+\\left(3\\right)^2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=\\left(3 t + 1\\right) e^{t}\\\\\n&=\\left(3 t + 1\\right) e^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the scalar by the speed and use the parameter bounds:\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{1}^{2} f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThus the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(\\left(3 t + 1\\right) e^{t}\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(3\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720437 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the triangle spanned by $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -4,-4,1\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 1,-2,5\\rangle$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Compute the cross product using a determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}&\\mathbf{j}&\\mathbf{k}\\\\-4 & -4 & 1\\\\1 & -2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=((-4)\\cdot (5)-(1)\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{i}\\\\&\\quad-((-4)\\cdot (5)-(1)\\cdot (1))\\mathbf{j}\\\\&\\quad+((-4)\\cdot (-2)-(-4)\\cdot (1))\\mathbf{k}\\\\&=\\langle -18,21,12\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude gives the corresponding parallelogram area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM&=\\|\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\|\\\\&=\\sqrt{(-18)^{2}+(21)^{2}+(12)^{2}}\\\\&=\\sqrt{909}=3\\sqrt{101}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle has half the area of the parallelogram.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA&=\\frac12 M=\\frac{3\\sqrt{101}}{2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{101}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720440 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the triangle spanned by $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -2,-3,2\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle -1,-2,-4\\rangle$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Compute the cross product using a determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}&\\mathbf{j}&\\mathbf{k}\\\\-2 & -3 & 2\\\\-1 & -2 & -4\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=((-3)\\cdot (-4)-(2)\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{i}\\\\&\\quad-((-2)\\cdot (-4)-(2)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{j}\\\\&\\quad+((-2)\\cdot (-2)-(-3)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{k}\\\\&=\\langle 16,-10,1\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude gives the corresponding parallelogram area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM&=\\|\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\|\\\\&=\\sqrt{(16)^{2}+(-10)^{2}+(1)^{2}}\\\\&=\\sqrt{357}=\\sqrt{357}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle has half the area of the parallelogram.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA&=\\frac12 M=\\frac{\\sqrt{357}}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{357}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720446 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the triangle spanned by $\\mathbf{u}=\\langle -1,-4,-3\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle 0,3,2\\rangle$. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "Compute the cross product using a determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}&\\mathbf{j}&\\mathbf{k}\\\\-1 & -4 & -3\\\\0 & 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=((-4)\\cdot (2)-(-3)\\cdot (3))\\mathbf{i}\\\\&\\quad-((-1)\\cdot (2)-(-3)\\cdot (0))\\mathbf{j}\\\\&\\quad+((-1)\\cdot (3)-(-4)\\cdot (0))\\mathbf{k}\\\\&=\\langle 1,2,-3\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude gives the corresponding parallelogram area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM&=\\|\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v}\\|\\\\&=\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(-3)^{2}}\\\\&=\\sqrt{14}=\\sqrt{14}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle has half the area of the parallelogram.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA&=\\frac12 M=\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720449 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the triangle spanned by $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 5,2,5\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{q}=\\langle 1,-4,-1\\rangle$. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "Compute the cross product using a determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{p}\\times \\mathbf{q}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}&\\mathbf{j}&\\mathbf{k}\\\\5 & 2 & 5\\\\1 & -4 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=((2)\\cdot (-1)-(5)\\cdot (-4))\\mathbf{i}\\\\&\\quad-((5)\\cdot (-1)-(5)\\cdot (1))\\mathbf{j}\\\\&\\quad+((5)\\cdot (-4)-(2)\\cdot (1))\\mathbf{k}\\\\&=\\langle 18,10,-22\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude gives the corresponding parallelogram area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM&=\\|\\mathbf{p}\\times \\mathbf{q}\\|\\\\&=\\sqrt{(18)^{2}+(10)^{2}+(-22)^{2}}\\\\&=\\sqrt{908}=2\\sqrt{227}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle has half the area of the parallelogram.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA&=\\frac12 M=\\frac{2\\sqrt{227}}{2}=\\sqrt{227}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720454 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the triangle spanned by $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 1,-2,3\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 5,-1,4\\rangle$. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "Compute the cross product using a determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}&\\mathbf{j}&\\mathbf{k}\\\\1 & -2 & 3\\\\5 & -1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=((-2)\\cdot (4)-(3)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{i}\\\\&\\quad-((1)\\cdot (4)-(3)\\cdot (5))\\mathbf{j}\\\\&\\quad+((1)\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot (5))\\mathbf{k}\\\\&=\\langle -5,11,9\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude gives the corresponding parallelogram area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM&=\\|\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\|\\\\&=\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+(11)^{2}+(9)^{2}}\\\\&=\\sqrt{227}=\\sqrt{227}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle has half the area of the parallelogram.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA&=\\frac12 M=\\frac{\\sqrt{227}}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{227}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720466 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overrightarrow{AB}$, $\\overrightarrow{AC}$, and $\\overrightarrow{AD}$, given\n\n$$\nA(1,-1,2),\\quad B(2,1,2),\\quad C(0,0,4),\\quad D(3,-1,3).\n$$\n\nSelect the volume in cubic units." :question/worked-solution "First find the displacement vectors from the common vertex $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{AB}&=\\langle2-1,1-(-1),2-2\\rangle=\\langle1,2,0\\rangle,\\\\\n\\overrightarrow{AC}&=\\langle0-1,0-(-1),4-2\\rangle=\\langle-1,1,2\\rangle,\\\\\n\\overrightarrow{AD}&=\\langle3-1,-1-(-1),3-2\\rangle=\\langle2,0,1\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $T=\\overrightarrow{AB}\\cdot(\\overrightarrow{AC}\\times\\overrightarrow{AD})$. Expand its determinant along the first row. The cofactor signs are $+,-,+$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT&=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=1(1\\cdot1-2\\cdot0)-2((-1)\\cdot1-2\\cdot2)+0\\\\\n&=1-2(-5)\\\\\n&=11.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nV=|11|=11\\text{ cubic units}.\n$$\n\nBoth nonzero cofactor contributions are needed. The middle contribution is positive because the cofactor sign and its minor are both negative. There is no division by six: that factor would give a tetrahedron's volume."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720470 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overrightarrow{AB}$, $\\overrightarrow{AC}$, and $\\overrightarrow{AD}$, given\n\n$$\nA(3,1,1),\\quad B(1,1,1),\\quad C(4,4,1),\\quad D(3,2,3).\n$$\n\nFirst subtract the coordinates of $A$ from each endpoint to find the three edge vectors. Then use\n\n$$\nV=\\left|\\overrightarrow{AB}\\cdot\\left(\\overrightarrow{AC}\\times\\overrightarrow{AD}\\right)\\right|.\n$$\n\nThe schematic shows the three edges meeting at a common vertex, with $\\mathbf{a}=\\overrightarrow{AB}$, $\\mathbf{b}=\\overrightarrow{AC}$, and $\\mathbf{c}=\\overrightarrow{AD}$.\n\n![](images/25/2597b48a010607fca71c551dbce8e9e2bbeda47d0892a582347ae1cdf19f1bf6.png)\n\nSelect the volume in cubic units." :question/worked-solution "Form each displacement vector by subtracting the common starting point $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{AB}&=\\langle 1-3,1-1,1-1\\rangle=\\langle-2,0,0\\rangle,\\\\\n\\overrightarrow{AC}&=\\langle 4-3,4-1,1-1\\rangle=\\langle1,3,0\\rangle,\\\\\n\\overrightarrow{AD}&=\\langle 3-3,2-1,3-1\\rangle=\\langle0,1,2\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scalar triple product is the determinant with these edge vectors as rows. Expanding along the first row, the two zero entries leave just one term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{AB}\\cdot(\\overrightarrow{AC}\\times\\overrightarrow{AD})\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=(-2)(3\\cdot2-0\\cdot1)\\\\\n&=-12.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nVolume is the absolute value of the triple product, so\n\n$$\nV=|-12|=12\\text{ cubic units}.\n$$\n\nThe negative triple product describes orientation; it does not give a negative volume. Using the position vectors of $B$, $C$, and $D$ without subtracting $A$ would give the wrong edges."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720471 :question/problem "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overrightarrow{AB}$, $\\overrightarrow{AC}$, and $\\overrightarrow{AD}$, given\n\n$$\nA(-1,2,-1),\\quad B(1,1,0),\\quad C(0,3,0),\\quad D(0,4,-3).\n$$\n\nEnter the volume in cubic units.\n\n$V=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Subtract $A$ from each endpoint to obtain the three edge vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{AB}&=\\langle1-(-1),1-2,0-(-1)\\rangle=\\langle2,-1,1\\rangle,\\\\\n\\overrightarrow{AC}&=\\langle0-(-1),3-2,0-(-1)\\rangle=\\langle1,1,1\\rangle,\\\\\n\\overrightarrow{AD}&=\\langle0-(-1),4-2,-3-(-1)\\rangle=\\langle1,2,-2\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $T=\\overrightarrow{AB}\\cdot(\\overrightarrow{AC}\\times\\overrightarrow{AD})$. All three first-row cofactor contributions are nonzero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT&=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=2(1\\cdot(-2)-1\\cdot2)\\\\\n&\\quad{}-(-1)(1\\cdot(-2)-1\\cdot1)\\\\\n&\\quad{}+1(1\\cdot2-1\\cdot1)\\\\\n&=2(-4)+(-3)+1\\\\\n&=-8-3+1\\\\\n&=-10.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required volume is the absolute value:\n\n$$\nV=|-10|=10\\text{ cubic units}.\n$$\n\nUsing the endpoint position vectors instead of the displacement vectors changes the solid. Taking the absolute value of each cofactor term separately is also incorrect: combine the signed contributions before taking the absolute value."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720486 :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F=\\ln(x+2y)\\,\\mathbf i+xe^{2y}\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F$ at the point $(1,0)$." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$, use\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F=\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate before evaluating at the point. The other coordinate is held constant in each derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F\n&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\ln(x+2y)+\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(xe^{2y})\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{x+2y}+2xe^{2y}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe logarithm requires $x+2y>0$, which holds at $(1,0)$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F)(1,0)\n&=\\frac{1}{1+2(0)}+2(1)e^{2(0)}\\\\\n&=1+2\\\\\n&=3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720491 :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F=xe^{xy}\\,\\mathbf i+y\\ln(x+y)\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F$ at the point $(2,1)$." :question/worked-solution "The divergence is a scalar:\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F=\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nBoth components require the product rule. In the first derivative, hold $y$ constant and use $\\partial(xy)/\\partial x=y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}\n&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(xe^{xy})\\\\\n&=e^{xy}+xye^{xy}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the second derivative, hold $x$ constant and differentiate the logarithm using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\n&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\bigl(y\\ln(x+y)\\bigr)\\\\\n&=\\ln(x+y)+\\frac{y}{x+y}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the derivatives before substituting. Since $2+1>0$, the point lies in the domain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F\n&=(1+xy)e^{xy}+\\ln(x+y)+\\frac{y}{x+y},\\\\\n(\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F)(2,1)\n&=(1+2\\cdot1)e^{2\\cdot1}+\\ln(2+1)+\\frac{1}{2+1}\\\\\n&=3e^2+\\ln 3+\\frac{1}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720497 :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F=3xy^2\\,\\mathbf i+2x^2y\\,\\mathbf j,\n$$\nwhat is $\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F$?" :question/worked-solution "For a two-dimensional vector field $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$, the divergence is the scalar field\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F=\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nTreat $y$ as constant in the first derivative and $x$ as constant in the second:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F\n&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(3xy^2)+\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(2x^2y)\\\\\n&=3y^2+2x^2\\\\\n&=2x^2+3y^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a scalar field, so no unit vectors remain."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720502 :question/problem "Let\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}&=\\langle -4,-1,3\\rangle,\\\\\n\\mathbf{b}&=\\langle 2,0,-4\\rangle,\\\\\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\langle -3,3,4\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by these three vectors.\n\n$V=${{volume}}" :question/worked-solution "The volume is the absolute value of the scalar triple product of the actual edge vectors. Use those edges as the determinant rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD&=\\begin{vmatrix}-4&-1&3\\\\2&0&-4\\\\-3&3&4\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=(-4)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0&-4\\\\3&4\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&\\quad -(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2&-4\\\\-3&4\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&\\quad +3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2&0\\\\-3&3\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=(-4)(0\\cdot 4-(-4)\\cdot 3)\\\\\n&\\quad -(-1)(2\\cdot 4-(-4)\\cdot (-3))\\\\\n&\\quad +3(2\\cdot 3-0\\cdot (-3))\\\\\n&=(-48)+(-4)+18=-34.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, take the absolute value:\n\n$$\nV=|-34|=34.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720503 :question/problem "Let\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}&=\\langle 2,2,-2\\rangle,\\\\\n\\mathbf{b}&=\\langle 4,0,-1\\rangle,\\\\\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\langle -3,-4,-4\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these three vectors." :question/worked-solution "The volume is the absolute value of the scalar triple product of the actual edge vectors. Use those edges as the determinant rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD&=\\begin{vmatrix}2&2&-2\\\\4&0&-1\\\\-3&-4&-4\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0&-1\\\\-4&-4\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&\\quad -2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4&-1\\\\-3&-4\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&\\quad +(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4&0\\\\-3&-4\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=2(0\\cdot (-4)-(-1)\\cdot (-4))\\\\\n&\\quad -2(4\\cdot (-4)-(-1)\\cdot (-3))\\\\\n&\\quad +(-2)(4\\cdot (-4)-0\\cdot (-3))\\\\\n&=(-8)+38+32=62.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, take the absolute value:\n\n$$\nV=|62|=62.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720524 :question/problem "Let\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}&=\\langle 3,-4,1\\rangle,\\\\\n\\mathbf{b}&=\\langle -1,2,-4\\rangle,\\\\\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\langle 2,-3,0\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these three vectors." :question/worked-solution "The volume is the absolute value of the scalar triple product of the actual edge vectors. Use those edges as the determinant rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD&=\\begin{vmatrix}3&-4&1\\\\-1&2&-4\\\\2&-3&0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2&-4\\\\-3&0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&\\quad -(-4)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1&-4\\\\2&0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&\\quad +1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1&2\\\\2&-3\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=3(2\\cdot 0-(-4)\\cdot (-3))\\\\\n&\\quad -(-4)((-1)\\cdot 0-(-4)\\cdot 2)\\\\\n&\\quad +1((-1)\\cdot (-3)-2\\cdot 2)\\\\\n&=(-36)+32+(-1)=-5.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, take the absolute value:\n\n$$\nV=|-5|=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720527 :question/problem "Let\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}&=\\langle 0,3,1\\rangle,\\\\\n\\mathbf{b}&=\\langle 4,-1,-2\\rangle,\\\\\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\langle -1,-4,0\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these three vectors." :question/worked-solution "The volume is the absolute value of the scalar triple product of the actual edge vectors. Use those edges as the determinant rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD&=\\begin{vmatrix}0&3&1\\\\4&-1&-2\\\\-1&-4&0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1&-2\\\\-4&0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&\\quad -3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4&-2\\\\-1&0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&\\quad +1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4&-1\\\\-1&-4\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=0((-1)\\cdot 0-(-2)\\cdot (-4))\\\\\n&\\quad -3(4\\cdot 0-(-2)\\cdot (-1))\\\\\n&\\quad +1(4\\cdot (-4)-(-1)\\cdot (-1))\\\\\n&=0+6+(-17)=-11.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, take the absolute value:\n\n$$\nV=|-11|=11.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720528 :question/problem "Let\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}&=\\langle -3,1,3\\rangle,\\\\\n\\mathbf{b}&=\\langle 3,0,1\\rangle,\\\\\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\langle -1,4,-3\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these three vectors." :question/worked-solution "The volume is the absolute value of the scalar triple product of the actual edge vectors. Use those edges as the determinant rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD&=\\begin{vmatrix}-3&1&3\\\\3&0&1\\\\-1&4&-3\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0&1\\\\4&-3\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&\\quad -1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3&1\\\\-1&-3\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&\\quad +3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3&0\\\\-1&4\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=(-3)(0\\cdot (-3)-1\\cdot 4)\\\\\n&\\quad -1(3\\cdot (-3)-1\\cdot (-1))\\\\\n&\\quad +3(3\\cdot 4-0\\cdot (-1))\\\\\n&=12+8+36=56.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, take the absolute value:\n\n$$\nV=|56|=56.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720533 :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $QH$ of $\\triangle PQR$, where $P(-2,-2,0)$, $Q(0,-2,1)$, $R(0,0,-3)$. Here $H$ is the foot of the perpendicular from $Q$ to the line $PR$." :question/worked-solution "The altitude $QH$ corresponds to the base $PR$, so\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A_{\\triangle PQR}=\\frac12 PR\\cdot QH.\n$$\n\nForm the required edge vectors from the vertices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overrightarrow{PR}&=\\langle 0-(-2),0-(-2),-3-0\\rangle,\\\\\n&=\\langle 2,2,-3\\rangle.\\\\\n\\overrightarrow{PQ}&=\\langle 0-(-2),-2-(-2),1-0\\rangle,\\\\\n&=\\langle 2,0,1\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overrightarrow{PR}\\times \\overrightarrow{PQ}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\2&2&-3\\\\2&0&1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\nw_1&=2\\cdot 1-(-3)\\cdot 0=2,\\\\\nw_2&=(-3)\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 1=-8,\\\\\nw_3&=2\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 2=-4,\\\\\n\\overrightarrow{PR}\\times \\overrightarrow{PQ}&=\\langle 2,-8,-4\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude of the cross product gives the parallelogram area:\n\n$$\n\\left|\\overrightarrow{PR}\\times \\overrightarrow{PQ}\\right|=\\sqrt{2^2+(-8)^2+(-4)^2}=2 \\sqrt{21}.\n$$\n\nThe matching base length is\n\n$$\nPR=\\sqrt{2^2+2^2+(-3)^2}=\\sqrt{17}.\n$$\n\nThe triangle has half the parallelogram area:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A_{\\triangle PQR}=\\frac12\\left(2 \\sqrt{21}\\right)=\\sqrt{21}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the area and matching base, then solve for the altitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{21}&=\\frac12\\left(\\sqrt{17}\\right)QH,\\\\\n2 \\sqrt{21}&=\\left(\\sqrt{17}\\right)QH,\\\\\nQH&=\\frac{2 \\sqrt{21}}{\\sqrt{17}}=\\frac{2 \\sqrt{357}}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe altitude is $\\frac{2 \\sqrt{357}}{17}$ units."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720539 :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $MH$ of $\\triangle LMN$, where $L(-2,0,1)$, $M(-4,3,2)$, $N(-5,-2,1)$. Here $H$ is the foot of the perpendicular from $M$ to the line $LN$." :question/worked-solution "The altitude $MH$ corresponds to the base $LN$, so\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A_{\\triangle LMN}=\\frac12 LN\\cdot MH.\n$$\n\nForm the required edge vectors from the vertices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overrightarrow{LN}&=\\langle -5-(-2),-2-0,1-1\\rangle,\\\\\n&=\\langle -3,-2,0\\rangle.\\\\\n\\overrightarrow{LM}&=\\langle -4-(-2),3-0,2-1\\rangle,\\\\\n&=\\langle -2,3,1\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overrightarrow{LN}\\times \\overrightarrow{LM}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\-3&-2&0\\\\-2&3&1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\nw_1&=(-2)\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 3=-2,\\\\\nw_2&=0\\cdot (-2)-(-3)\\cdot 1=3,\\\\\nw_3&=(-3)\\cdot 3-(-2)\\cdot (-2)=-13,\\\\\n\\overrightarrow{LN}\\times \\overrightarrow{LM}&=\\langle -2,3,-13\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude of the cross product gives the parallelogram area:\n\n$$\n\\left|\\overrightarrow{LN}\\times \\overrightarrow{LM}\\right|=\\sqrt{(-2)^2+3^2+(-13)^2}=\\sqrt{182}.\n$$\n\nThe matching base length is\n\n$$\nLN=\\sqrt{(-3)^2+(-2)^2+0^2}=\\sqrt{13}.\n$$\n\nThe triangle has half the parallelogram area:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A_{\\triangle LMN}=\\frac12\\left(\\sqrt{182}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{182}}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the area and matching base, then solve for the altitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{182}}{2}&=\\frac12\\left(\\sqrt{13}\\right)MH,\\\\\n\\sqrt{182}&=\\left(\\sqrt{13}\\right)MH,\\\\\nMH&=\\frac{\\sqrt{182}}{\\sqrt{13}}=\\sqrt{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe altitude is $\\sqrt{14}$ units."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720549 :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $NH$ of $\\triangle LMN$, where $L(-2,-2,2)$, $M(1,-1,-1)$, $N(-1,0,4)$. Here $H$ is the foot of the perpendicular from $N$ to the line $LM$." :question/worked-solution "The altitude $NH$ corresponds to the base $LM$, so\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A_{\\triangle LMN}=\\frac12 LM\\cdot NH.\n$$\n\nForm the required edge vectors from the vertices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overrightarrow{LM}&=\\langle 1-(-2),-1-(-2),-1-2\\rangle,\\\\\n&=\\langle 3,1,-3\\rangle.\\\\\n\\overrightarrow{LN}&=\\langle -1-(-2),0-(-2),4-2\\rangle,\\\\\n&=\\langle 1,2,2\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overrightarrow{LM}\\times \\overrightarrow{LN}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\3&1&-3\\\\1&2&2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\nw_1&=1\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot 2=8,\\\\\nw_2&=(-3)\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2=-9,\\\\\nw_3&=3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1=5,\\\\\n\\overrightarrow{LM}\\times \\overrightarrow{LN}&=\\langle 8,-9,5\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude of the cross product gives the parallelogram area:\n\n$$\n\\left|\\overrightarrow{LM}\\times \\overrightarrow{LN}\\right|=\\sqrt{8^2+(-9)^2+5^2}=\\sqrt{170}.\n$$\n\nThe matching base length is\n\n$$\nLM=\\sqrt{3^2+1^2+(-3)^2}=\\sqrt{19}.\n$$\n\nThe triangle has half the parallelogram area:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A_{\\triangle LMN}=\\frac12\\left(\\sqrt{170}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{170}}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the area and matching base, then solve for the altitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{170}}{2}&=\\frac12\\left(\\sqrt{19}\\right)NH,\\\\\n\\sqrt{170}&=\\left(\\sqrt{19}\\right)NH,\\\\\nNH&=\\frac{\\sqrt{170}}{\\sqrt{19}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3230}}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe altitude is $\\frac{\\sqrt{3230}}{19}$ units."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720554 :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $LH$ of $\\triangle LMN$, where $L(0,-1,-2)$, $M(2,0,1)$, $N(1,-1,1)$. Here $H$ is the foot of the perpendicular from $L$ to the line $MN$." :question/worked-solution "The altitude $LH$ corresponds to the base $MN$, so\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A_{\\triangle LMN}=\\frac12 MN\\cdot LH.\n$$\n\nForm the required edge vectors from the vertices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overrightarrow{MN}&=\\langle 1-2,-1-0,1-1\\rangle,\\\\\n&=\\langle -1,-1,0\\rangle.\\\\\n\\overrightarrow{ML}&=\\langle 0-2,-1-0,-2-1\\rangle,\\\\\n&=\\langle -2,-1,-3\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overrightarrow{MN}\\times \\overrightarrow{ML}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\-1&-1&0\\\\-2&-1&-3\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\nw_1&=(-1)\\cdot (-3)-0\\cdot (-1)=3,\\\\\nw_2&=0\\cdot (-2)-(-1)\\cdot (-3)=-3,\\\\\nw_3&=(-1)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-2)=-1,\\\\\n\\overrightarrow{MN}\\times \\overrightarrow{ML}&=\\langle 3,-3,-1\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude of the cross product gives the parallelogram area:\n\n$$\n\\left|\\overrightarrow{MN}\\times \\overrightarrow{ML}\\right|=\\sqrt{3^2+(-3)^2+(-1)^2}=\\sqrt{19}.\n$$\n\nThe matching base length is\n\n$$\nMN=\\sqrt{(-1)^2+(-1)^2+0^2}=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThe triangle has half the parallelogram area:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A_{\\triangle LMN}=\\frac12\\left(\\sqrt{19}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the area and matching base, then solve for the altitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{2}&=\\frac12\\left(\\sqrt{2}\\right)LH,\\\\\n\\sqrt{19}&=\\left(\\sqrt{2}\\right)LH,\\\\\nLH&=\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{38}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe altitude is $\\frac{\\sqrt{38}}{2}$ units."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720558 :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $NH$ of $\\triangle LMN$, where $L(-1,0,0)$, $M(0,-3,-3)$, $N(-3,-2,1)$. Here $H$ is the foot of the perpendicular from $N$ to the line $LM$." :question/worked-solution "The altitude $NH$ corresponds to the base $LM$, so\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A_{\\triangle LMN}=\\frac12 LM\\cdot NH.\n$$\n\nForm the required edge vectors from the vertices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overrightarrow{LM}&=\\langle 0-(-1),-3-0,-3-0\\rangle,\\\\\n&=\\langle 1,-3,-3\\rangle.\\\\\n\\overrightarrow{LN}&=\\langle -3-(-1),-2-0,1-0\\rangle,\\\\\n&=\\langle -2,-2,1\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overrightarrow{LM}\\times \\overrightarrow{LN}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\1&-3&-3\\\\-2&-2&1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\nw_1&=(-3)\\cdot 1-(-3)\\cdot (-2)=-9,\\\\\nw_2&=(-3)\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 1=5,\\\\\nw_3&=1\\cdot (-2)-(-3)\\cdot (-2)=-8,\\\\\n\\overrightarrow{LM}\\times \\overrightarrow{LN}&=\\langle -9,5,-8\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude of the cross product gives the parallelogram area:\n\n$$\n\\left|\\overrightarrow{LM}\\times \\overrightarrow{LN}\\right|=\\sqrt{(-9)^2+5^2+(-8)^2}=\\sqrt{170}.\n$$\n\nThe matching base length is\n\n$$\nLM=\\sqrt{1^2+(-3)^2+(-3)^2}=\\sqrt{19}.\n$$\n\nThe triangle has half the parallelogram area:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A_{\\triangle LMN}=\\frac12\\left(\\sqrt{170}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{170}}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the area and matching base, then solve for the altitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{170}}{2}&=\\frac12\\left(\\sqrt{19}\\right)NH,\\\\\n\\sqrt{170}&=\\left(\\sqrt{19}\\right)NH,\\\\\nNH&=\\frac{\\sqrt{170}}{\\sqrt{19}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3230}}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe altitude is $\\frac{\\sqrt{3230}}{19}$ units."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720573 :question/problem "Calculate the exact area of triangle $DEF$ with vertices $D(0,-3,4)$, $E(-2,1,2)$, $F(-3,0,3)$, in square units." :question/worked-solution "Form two sides with the same starting vertex:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{FD}&=\\langle 0-(-3),-3-0,4-3\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle 3,-3,1\\rangle \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{FE}&=\\langle -2-(-3),1-0,2-3\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle 1,1,-1\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCompute the cross product:\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{FD}\\times \\overrightarrow{FE}=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}&\\mathbf{j}&\\mathbf{k}\\\\3 & -3 & 1\\\\1 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nw_1&=(-3)\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 1=2 \\\\\nw_2&=1\\cdot 1-3\\cdot (-1)=4 \\\\\nw_3&=3\\cdot 1-(-3)\\cdot 1=6 \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{FD}\\times \\overrightarrow{FE}&=\\langle 2,4,6\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude is the parallelogram's area:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lVert \\overrightarrow{FD}\\times \\overrightarrow{FE}\\rVert&=\\sqrt{2^2+4^2+6^2} \\\\\n&=\\sqrt{56}=2 \\sqrt{14}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle has half that area:\n$$\nT=\\frac12\\lVert \\overrightarrow{FD}\\times \\overrightarrow{FE}\\rVert=\\frac{2 \\sqrt{14}}{2}=\\sqrt{14}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720579 :question/problem "Calculate the exact area of triangle $RST$ with vertices $R(0,-3,3)$, $S(2,0,0)$, $T(-2,-5,6)$, in square units.\n\n$T=$ {{area}}" :question/worked-solution "Form two sides with the same starting vertex:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{RS}&=\\langle 2-0,0-(-3),0-3\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle 2,3,-3\\rangle \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{RT}&=\\langle -2-0,-5-(-3),6-3\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle -2,-2,3\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCompute the cross product:\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{RS}\\times \\overrightarrow{RT}=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}&\\mathbf{j}&\\mathbf{k}\\\\2 & 3 & -3\\\\-2 & -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nw_1&=3\\cdot 3-(-3)\\cdot (-2)=3 \\\\\nw_2&=(-3)\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 3=0 \\\\\nw_3&=2\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot (-2)=2 \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{RS}\\times \\overrightarrow{RT}&=\\langle 3,0,2\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude is the parallelogram's area:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lVert \\overrightarrow{RS}\\times \\overrightarrow{RT}\\rVert&=\\sqrt{3^2+0^2+2^2} \\\\\n&=\\sqrt{13}=\\sqrt{13}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle has half that area:\n$$\nT=\\frac12\\lVert \\overrightarrow{RS}\\times \\overrightarrow{RT}\\rVert=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720588 :question/problem "Calculate the exact area of triangle $ABC$ with vertices $A(-1,2,0)$, $B(0,1,-6)$, $C(-2,-1,-3)$, in square units." :question/worked-solution "Form two sides with the same starting vertex:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{CA}&=\\langle -1-(-2),2-(-1),0-(-3)\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle 1,3,3\\rangle \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{CB}&=\\langle 0-(-2),1-(-1),-6-(-3)\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle 2,2,-3\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCompute the cross product:\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{CA}\\times \\overrightarrow{CB}=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}&\\mathbf{j}&\\mathbf{k}\\\\1 & 3 & 3\\\\2 & 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nw_1&=3\\cdot (-3)-3\\cdot 2=-15 \\\\\nw_2&=3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-3)=9 \\\\\nw_3&=1\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 2=-4 \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{CA}\\times \\overrightarrow{CB}&=\\langle -15,9,-4\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude is the parallelogram's area:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lVert \\overrightarrow{CA}\\times \\overrightarrow{CB}\\rVert&=\\sqrt{(-15)^2+9^2+(-4)^2} \\\\\n&=\\sqrt{322}=\\sqrt{322}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle has half that area:\n$$\nT=\\frac12\\lVert \\overrightarrow{CA}\\times \\overrightarrow{CB}\\rVert=\\frac{\\sqrt{322}}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{322}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720592 :question/problem "Calculate the exact area of triangle $DEF$ with vertices $D(0,3,-2)$, $E(1,-1,-1)$, $F(-2,2,0)$, in square units." :question/worked-solution "Form two sides with the same starting vertex:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{FD}&=\\langle 0-(-2),3-2,-2-0\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle 2,1,-2\\rangle \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{FE}&=\\langle 1-(-2),-1-2,-1-0\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle 3,-3,-1\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCompute the cross product:\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{FD}\\times \\overrightarrow{FE}=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}&\\mathbf{j}&\\mathbf{k}\\\\2 & 1 & -2\\\\3 & -3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nw_1&=1\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot (-3)=-7 \\\\\nw_2&=(-2)\\cdot 3-2\\cdot (-1)=-4 \\\\\nw_3&=2\\cdot (-3)-1\\cdot 3=-9 \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{FD}\\times \\overrightarrow{FE}&=\\langle -7,-4,-9\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude is the parallelogram's area:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lVert \\overrightarrow{FD}\\times \\overrightarrow{FE}\\rVert&=\\sqrt{(-7)^2+(-4)^2+(-9)^2} \\\\\n&=\\sqrt{146}=\\sqrt{146}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle has half that area:\n$$\nT=\\frac12\\lVert \\overrightarrow{FD}\\times \\overrightarrow{FE}\\rVert=\\frac{\\sqrt{146}}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{146}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720597 :question/problem "Calculate the exact area of triangle $LMN$ with vertices $L(-3,-5,-2)$, $M(-3,-4,0)$, $N(-1,-2,1)$, in square units." :question/worked-solution "Form two sides with the same starting vertex:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{NL}&=\\langle -3-(-1),-5-(-2),-2-1\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle -2,-3,-3\\rangle \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{NM}&=\\langle -3-(-1),-4-(-2),0-1\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle -2,-2,-1\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCompute the cross product:\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{NL}\\times \\overrightarrow{NM}=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}&\\mathbf{j}&\\mathbf{k}\\\\-2 & -3 & -3\\\\-2 & -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nw_1&=(-3)\\cdot (-1)-(-3)\\cdot (-2)=-3 \\\\\nw_2&=(-3)\\cdot (-2)-(-2)\\cdot (-1)=4 \\\\\nw_3&=(-2)\\cdot (-2)-(-3)\\cdot (-2)=-2 \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{NL}\\times \\overrightarrow{NM}&=\\langle -3,4,-2\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude is the parallelogram's area:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lVert \\overrightarrow{NL}\\times \\overrightarrow{NM}\\rVert&=\\sqrt{(-3)^2+4^2+(-2)^2} \\\\\n&=\\sqrt{29}=\\sqrt{29}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle has half that area:\n$$\nT=\\frac12\\lVert \\overrightarrow{NL}\\times \\overrightarrow{NM}\\rVert=\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720603 :question/problem "Let\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}&=\\langle -2,3,-2\\rangle,\\\\\n\\mathbf{b}&=\\langle 3,-3,-1\\rangle,\\\\\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\langle 2,2,3\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped whose three edge vectors are\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b},\\quad -\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{c},\\quad \\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{c}.\n$$\n\n$V=${{volume}}" :question/worked-solution "First form the actual edge vectors component by component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{u}&=\\mathbf{b}\\\\\n&=\\langle 3,(-3),(-1)\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 3,-3,-1\\rangle.\\\\\n\\mathbf{v}&=-\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{c}\\\\\n&=\\langle -(-2)+2,-3+2,-(-2)+3\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 4,-1,5\\rangle.\\\\\n\\mathbf{w}&=\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{c}\\\\\n&=\\langle (-2)+2,3+2,(-2)+3\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 0,5,1\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe volume is the absolute value of the scalar triple product of the actual edge vectors. Use those edges as the determinant rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD&=\\begin{vmatrix}3&-3&-1\\\\4&-1&5\\\\0&5&1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1&5\\\\5&1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&\\quad -(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4&5\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&\\quad +(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4&-1\\\\0&5\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=3((-1)\\cdot 1-5\\cdot 5)\\\\\n&\\quad -(-3)(4\\cdot 1-5\\cdot 0)\\\\\n&\\quad +(-1)(4\\cdot 5-(-1)\\cdot 0)\\\\\n&=(-78)+12+(-20)=-86.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, take the absolute value:\n\n$$\nV=|-86|=86.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720607 :question/problem "Let\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}&=\\langle -3,0,0\\rangle,\\\\\n\\mathbf{b}&=\\langle 2,2,-2\\rangle,\\\\\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\langle 1,-2,3\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped whose three edge vectors are\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a},\\quad \\mathbf{b}+\\mathbf{c},\\quad -\\mathbf{b}+\\mathbf{c}.\n$$\n\n$V=${{volume}}" :question/worked-solution "First form the actual edge vectors component by component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{u}&=\\mathbf{a}\\\\\n&=\\langle (-3),0,0\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle -3,0,0\\rangle.\\\\\n\\mathbf{v}&=\\mathbf{b}+\\mathbf{c}\\\\\n&=\\langle 2+1,2+(-2),(-2)+3\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 3,0,1\\rangle.\\\\\n\\mathbf{w}&=-\\mathbf{b}+\\mathbf{c}\\\\\n&=\\langle -2+1,-2+(-2),-(-2)+3\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle -1,-4,5\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe volume is the absolute value of the scalar triple product of the actual edge vectors. Use those edges as the determinant rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD&=\\begin{vmatrix}-3&0&0\\\\3&0&1\\\\-1&-4&5\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0&1\\\\-4&5\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&\\quad -0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3&1\\\\-1&5\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&\\quad +0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3&0\\\\-1&-4\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=(-3)(0\\cdot 5-1\\cdot (-4))\\\\\n&\\quad -0(3\\cdot 5-1\\cdot (-1))\\\\\n&\\quad +0(3\\cdot (-4)-0\\cdot (-1))\\\\\n&=(-12)+0+0=-12.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, take the absolute value:\n\n$$\nV=|-12|=12.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720611 :question/problem "Let\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}&=\\langle 2,1,-3\\rangle,\\\\\n\\mathbf{b}&=\\langle 3,-2,0\\rangle,\\\\\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\langle -2,0,-1\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped whose three edge vectors are\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{c},\\quad \\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{c},\\quad \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\n$V=${{volume}}" :question/worked-solution "First form the actual edge vectors component by component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{u}&=\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{c}\\\\\n&=\\langle 2+(-2),1+0,(-3)+(-1)\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 0,1,-4\\rangle.\\\\\n\\mathbf{v}&=\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{c}\\\\\n&=\\langle 2-(-2),1-0,(-3)-(-1)\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 4,1,-2\\rangle.\\\\\n\\mathbf{w}&=\\mathbf{b}\\\\\n&=\\langle 3,(-2),0\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 3,-2,0\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe volume is the absolute value of the scalar triple product of the actual edge vectors. Use those edges as the determinant rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD&=\\begin{vmatrix}0&1&-4\\\\4&1&-2\\\\3&-2&0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1&-2\\\\-2&0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&\\quad -1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4&-2\\\\3&0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&\\quad +(-4)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4&1\\\\3&-2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=0(1\\cdot 0-(-2)\\cdot (-2))\\\\\n&\\quad -1(4\\cdot 0-(-2)\\cdot 3)\\\\\n&\\quad +(-4)(4\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 3)\\\\\n&=0+(-6)+44=38.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, take the absolute value:\n\n$$\nV=|38|=38.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720613 :question/problem "Let\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}&=\\langle 0,0,1\\rangle,\\\\\n\\mathbf{b}&=\\langle 3,-1,0\\rangle,\\\\\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\langle 1,-2,2\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped whose three edge vectors are\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{c},\\quad \\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b},\\quad \\mathbf{c}.\n$$\n\n$V=${{volume}}" :question/worked-solution "First form the actual edge vectors component by component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{u}&=\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{c}\\\\\n&=\\langle 3-1,(-1)-(-2),0-2\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 2,1,-2\\rangle.\\\\\n\\mathbf{v}&=\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b}\\\\\n&=\\langle 0-3,0-(-1),1-0\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle -3,1,1\\rangle.\\\\\n\\mathbf{w}&=\\mathbf{c}\\\\\n&=\\langle 1,(-2),2\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 1,-2,2\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe volume is the absolute value of the scalar triple product of the actual edge vectors. Use those edges as the determinant rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD&=\\begin{vmatrix}2&1&-2\\\\-3&1&1\\\\1&-2&2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1&1\\\\-2&2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&\\quad -1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3&1\\\\1&2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&\\quad +(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3&1\\\\1&-2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=2(1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-2))\\\\\n&\\quad -1((-3)\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1)\\\\\n&\\quad +(-2)((-3)\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 1)\\\\\n&=8+7+(-10)=5.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, take the absolute value:\n\n$$\nV=|5|=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720616 :question/problem "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface defined by the equations\n\n$$\nx=u+v,\\qquad y=u^2+v,\\qquad z=uv.\n$$\n" :question/worked-solution "For a parametrization $\\mathbf r(u,v)$, the fundamental vector product is $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$, in that order. Differentiate with respect to one parameter while holding the other fixed.\n\nIn vector form, $\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle u+v,\\,u^2+v,\\,uv\\rangle$. The tangent vectors are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u&=\\langle 1,\\,2u,\\,v\\rangle,\\\\\\mathbf r_v&=\\langle 1,\\,1,\\,u\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i & \\mathbf j & \\mathbf k \\\\ 1 & 2u & v \\\\ 1 & 1 & u\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEach component subtracts two nonzero products. In particular, the middle cofactor has a minus sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)_1&=(2u)u-v\\cdot1=2u^2-v,\\\\(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)_2&=-(1\\cdot u-v\\cdot1)=v-u,\\\\(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)_3&=1\\cdot1-(2u)1=1-2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\langle 2u^2-v,\\,v-u,\\,1-2u\\rangle.\n$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720621 :question/problem "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface generated by the vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle u+v,\\,uv,\\,u\\sin(uv)\\rangle.\n$$\n" :question/worked-solution "For a parametrization $\\mathbf r(u,v)$, the fundamental vector product is $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$, in that order. Differentiate with respect to one parameter while holding the other fixed.\n\nThe third coordinate requires the product rule when differentiating with respect to $u$, and the chain rule for $\\sin(uv)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}\\bigl(u\\sin(uv)\\bigr)&=\\sin(uv)+uv\\cos(uv),\\\\\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}\\bigl(u\\sin(uv)\\bigr)&=u^2\\cos(uv).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the tangent vectors are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u&=\\langle 1,\\,v,\\,\\sin(uv)+uv\\cos(uv)\\rangle,\\\\\\mathbf r_v&=\\langle 1,\\,u,\\,u^2\\cos(uv)\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nForm the ordered determinant:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i & \\mathbf j & \\mathbf k \\\\ 1 & v & \\sin(uv)+uv\\cos(uv) \\\\ 1 & u & u^2\\cos(uv)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nExpand and simplify one component at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)_1&=u^2v\\cos(uv)-u\\bigl(\\sin(uv)+uv\\cos(uv)\\bigr)\\\\&=-u\\sin(uv),\\\\(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)_2&=-\\bigl(u^2\\cos(uv)-\\sin(uv)-uv\\cos(uv)\\bigr)\\\\&=\\sin(uv)+u(v-u)\\cos(uv),\\\\(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)_3&=1\\cdot u-v\\cdot1=u-v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\langle -u\\sin(uv),\\,\\sin(uv)+u(v-u)\\cos(uv),\\,u-v\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe first component retains the product-rule term after the two $u^2v\\cos(uv)$ terms cancel."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720631 :question/problem "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface defined by the equations\n\n$$\nx=u,\\qquad y=v,\\qquad z=uv.\n$$\n" :question/worked-solution "For a parametrization $\\mathbf r(u,v)$, the fundamental vector product is $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$, in that order. Differentiate with respect to one parameter while holding the other fixed.\n\nIn vector form, the surface is $\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle u,\\,v,\\,uv\\rangle$. The tangent vectors to its grid curves are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u&=\\langle 1,\\,0,\\,v\\rangle,\\\\\\mathbf r_v&=\\langle 0,\\,1,\\,u\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTheir ordered cross product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i & \\mathbf j & \\mathbf k \\\\ 1 & 0 & v \\\\ 0 & 1 & u\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\langle 0\\cdot u-v\\cdot1,\\,-(1\\cdot u-v\\cdot0),\\,1\\cdot1-0\\cdot0\\rangle\\\\&=\\langle -v,\\,-u,\\,1\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720646 :question/problem "Suppose $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})=-10$. Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped whose three edge vectors are $-2\\mathbf{c}$, $\\mathbf{b}$, $3\\mathbf{a}$.\n\n$V=${{volume}}" :question/worked-solution "The volume is the magnitude of the scalar triple product of the actual edges. Factor each edge scale out separately. An odd number of pairwise swaps reverses the triple product sign.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD&=(-2\\mathbf{c})\\cdot((\\mathbf{b})\\times(3\\mathbf{a}))\\\\\n&=-6\\bigl[\\mathbf{c}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a})\\bigr]\\\\\n&=-6\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (-10)=-60.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTake the absolute value:\n\n$$\nV=|-60|=60.\n$$"}]}
